module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_field_read_on_an_unestablished_element_type_is_accepted_through_its_slot

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

data a_field_read_on_an_unestablished_element_type_is_accepted_through_its_slot: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_field_read_on_an_unestablished_element_type_is_accepted_through_its_slot" as NonEmptyStr,
  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundBelowTheLadder, ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed, blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt, trigger_capability: "a generic binding that an uninformative actual (the [] or empty_map() placeholder) never takes first-wins, so the read sees either its established element type or the placeholder the existing guard refuses -- bright-pike-492's unify_lambda_solves follow-up after gunbc#14750 -- with this specimen's undeclared-field read enrolled as a RED and its declared-field read as the positive control." } },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a value whose element type no one established -- a List typed by an empty `[]` literal, whose element slot holds the inference placeholder TypeVariable(empty_list_element) -- reaches a field read through a generic callee's lambda, and v1.compiler.infer ACCEPTS the read whether or not any record declares the field. The guard written for exactly this case (is_never_constrained_enclosing_generic_base: deferring a read of empty_list_element 'would accept a field of a type nobody established') never sees it, because the substitution binds the callee's type variable to the application's element SLOT node, which is not itself a type variable.",
    "SPECIMEN (seed built from main 557a7efac39, bold-koi-381, 2026-10-25, gunbc compile --entry on fixture modules): `type Row { tags: List<String> }`, local generics `fl<T, A>(xs: List<T>, empty: A, cons: fn(A, T) -> A) -> A` and `snoc<T>(xs: List<T>, item: T) -> List<T>`; `let rows = fl(xs: xs, empty: [], cons: fn(acc, r) { snoc(xs: acc, item: r) })` then `fl(xs: rows, empty: 0, cons: fn(n, r) { n + length(xs: r.nope) })` -- `nope` is declared by nothing -- compiles with no blocking diagnostic (it stops only at the emitted-workspace write). DISCRIMINATING CONTROL: the same module with `let rows: List<Row> = ..` REFUSES the read (1 blocking error). The declared field `r.tags` through the unannotated let compiles. In the corpus: the same shape at v2.compiler.source_root_eval (modules = fold_list(xs: sources, empty: [], ..), then m.declarations / m.module / m.path) and v2.compiler.type_declaration_use_census (edge.label / edge.target) -- nine reads accepted on main whose element type is the placeholder.",
    "HARM: silent wrongness (DESIGN section 5) -- an undeclared field read is Accepted, and nothing refuses until the emitted target compiles or the value is consumed.",
    "HOW IT SURFACED: gunbc#15132's first head made unify_generic_child_pairs bind the slot's held type instead of the slot; the placeholder then reached the guard, which refused all nine corpus reads, and v2-bootstrap failed at emit (job 121128203061). The reads are not wrong programs -- their element type IS established by the fold's cons step -- so the corpus is correct and the seed has never typed them: on main they pass for the wrong reason.",
    "ROOT: v1.compiler.infer unify_generics binds a type variable FIRST-WINS, so the uninformative placeholder from `empty: []` (or `init: empty_map()`) takes the binding and the informative actual that follows (the cons step's item) never does. The repair is owned by bright-pike-492's unify_lambda_solves follow-up, which lands after gunbc#14750 (keen-heron-809's routing; smart-tern-402, who first proposed it, stood down). With it, the reads above are typed at their real element type, and an undeclared one refuses at the guard as intended.",
    "V2 STANDING FOR THIS CLASS (same source through v2.test.claim.body_let_annotation bla_assemble_with_peers then v2.compiler.infer infer, bold-koi-381, 2026-10-25): v2 FAILS CLOSED -- the undeclared read refuses as infer_reason_projection_receiver_declares_no_fields, and the annotated control as infer_reason_field_not_declared_on_receiver; v2 carries no silent acceptance, so the class does not exist on the v2 path.",
    "ADJACENT, NOT THIS CLASS: v2 also refuses the DECLARED read through the unannotated []-seeded let (projection_receiver_declares_no_fields). That is a false refusal of the valid twin, not a silent acceptance, and it does not lower this class's v2 standing: it is the known gap where an unannotated lambda or accumulator is typed only by an uninformative init (stern-cat-126's handoff of gunbc#15038, open item 2), mitigatable until that unification lands.",
    "RELATED CLASSES, one placeholder and distinct invalid states: gunbc.recurring_failure_mode unsolved_placeholder_reads_back_as_unit is the same empty_list_element placeholder reaching a field read that REFUSES but names Unit (a misattributed refusal, where this row is a silent acceptance); gunbc.recurring_failure_mode unapplied_generic_accumulator_types_element_as_container is the fold accumulator read off an unapplied expansion. The shared link is the fold carrier fixed by an uninformative init: gunbc#15022 (bright-pike-492) closes an open carrier, [] included, at the step's produced type in the seed, and gunbc#14411 records the first-wins reading of the same placeholder. They are related links, not this row's trigger, which is stated in the rung field.",
    "RUNG: found BELOW THE LADDER on the seed path (a silent acceptance) and still there. CEILING: STRUCTURALLY GUARANTEED -- the receiver's unsolved element type is a modeled state (TypeVariable empty_list_element) and the field read is decidable against it: once the binding carries either the established element type or the placeholder itself, the existing guard (is_never_constrained_enclosing_generic_base) refuses an undeclared read, so no Accepted program contains one. Not structurally impossible: the source can still write the read. NEXT TRIGGER: the rung field.",
  ],
  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.infer", decl_name: "unify_generics"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.infer", decl_name: "is_never_constrained_enclosing_generic_base"),
  ],
}
